<Base title="About" description="About Joshua Brancazio: how they work, what they do outside work, and what they are working toward.">
  <h1>About</h1>
  <p>
    I grew up in the Rhineland in Germany, and I want to take my kids back there someday. Family is
    a big part of who I am. My wife, our two kids, and our two dogs are a lot of the reason I do what
    I do, and a lot of the reason I automate things. Every small tool on this site is a little time I
    get back with them.
  </p>
  <p>
    I am an introvert. I tend to build something first and then go talk to people about it.
  </p>

  <h2>How I work</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Start with the business question. Before any analysis, I want to know what decision it is for.</li>
    <li>Better but not perfect is a fine place to ship, as long as I say what is not perfect yet.</li>
    <li>I will not put a number in front of leadership that I cannot stand behind.</li>
    <li>
      Speed comes from clear intake and clear scope, not from skipping steps. Ambiguous scope is what
      actually slows a team down.
    </li>
    <li>
      Keep process light. Map how things work today and agree on what done means before changing
      anything.
    </li>
    <li>AI is great for grunt work and first drafts. The judgment stays with a person, and someone checks the output.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One example. My team used story points for a while, but our tickets come in very different
    shapes. Some are quick access requests, some stall waiting on another team, and some mean
    learning something new first. I started to suspect the points were setting expectations we could
    not meet. So I started looking at cycle time by ticket type instead, which means being honest
    about wait time and defining done for each type.
  </p>

  <h2>Outside work</h2>
  <p>
    Ten years of hospitality did not wear off. I brew on an AeroPress and run blind cuppings at home.
    My wife jokes that I turned her into a snob, and at this point she critiques the brews more than I
    do.
  </p>
  <p>
    Most of my <a href="/projects/">side projects</a> start as a hobby: fantasy hockey, a World Cup
    pool, Balatro, the kitchen. The hobby is usually the excuse to learn the tool.
  </p>
  <p>
    I like working with my hands too. Home DIY, cooking, hunting, foraging, and car camping. For games
    it is Stardew Valley, Old School RuneScape, and Balatro.
  </p>
  <p>
    I am on the board of a local school foundation. I keep the operating manual for the carousel it
    runs at the farmers market and help school groups sign up and fundraise with it.
  </p>
  <p>
    I get excited by new shiny things. I am working on finishing one before I start the next.
  </p>

  <h2>What I am working toward</h2>
  <p>
    Growing into a director-level analytics role. Getting to know more people who lead data and
    analytics teams. Figuring out what responsible AI looks like in BI. Keeping up my nonprofit and
    education volunteering. And staying open to the right opportunity.
  </p>
  <p>
    The best way to reach me is <a href={site.links.linkedin}>LinkedIn</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
